<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-glutamate degradation IV" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4321" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE| (GABA) is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian brain. Recent findings suggest that GABA has a role as a signal molecule in plants as well |CITS: [15003233]|. In both animals and plants GABA is produced by the cytosolic enzyme |FRAME: AT5G17330-MONOMER|. GABA is degraded to |FRAME: SUC| by the enzymes |FRAME: AT3G22200-LER-MONOMER| and |FRAME:CPLX-6121|. The transamination of (GABA) with |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| to form |FRAME:SUCC-S-ALD| was first discovered in mammalian brain |CITS: [13044808]|. The action of these three enzymes combined defines a short pathway known as the GABA shunt, since it channels glutamate into the the |FRAME: TCA| bypassing two steps of that cycle. This eukaryotic pathway starts at the cytosol, where GABA is formed from glutamate. GABA then enters the mytochondrion, where the other two enzymes operate |CITS: [15003233]|. An alternative route of converting succinic semialdehyde is via the cytosolic enzyme &amp;gamma;-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase. The role of the alternative product 4-hydroxybutyrate in plants is unclear. While mammals only have 2-oxoglutarate-dependent GABA-transaminase (see |FRAME:GLUDEG-I-PWY|), both 2-oxoglutarate-dependent and pyruvate-dependent GABA-transaminase activities have been detected in crude plant extracts. The physiological roles and significance of this pathway include: 1) Assimilates carbons from glutamate to succinate which then enters the TCA cycle and thus functions as part of the mechanism plants use to maintain the carbon-nitrogen balance; 2) Protects plants from oxidative stress by providing the mitochondrial respiratory chain with NADH and succinate, generated in the succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase reaction; 3) Glutamate decarboxylase (GAD) is activated by acidic pH, and that the reaction catalyzed by GAD removes protons suggesting the pathway may also regulate the cytosolic pH balance; 4) The intermediate metabolite &amp;gamma;-aminobutyrate (GABA) is speculated to function as a nitrogen storage form, a deterrent of plant pathogen, an osmo-regulator, and a plant signal molecule involved in pollen tube growth. For a comprehensive review see |CITS: [15003233]|.</Description>
